          "question": "My cholesterol came back at 6.4.",
          "answer": "A single cholesterol level like 6.4 is usually considered as part of an overall assessment of your heart and blood vessel disease risk, rather than an urgent situation on its own.\n\nAccording to the \"Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation\" guideline, decisions about cholesterol are \"driven by estimated 10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk, not by the LDL number alone.\" This means that clinicians look at many factors together, not just one number, to understand your risk and decide on any next steps.\n\nFactors that contribute to this risk include smoking, high blood pressure, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, diet, and family history of heart disease, as noted in the \"Coronary artery disease\" document.\n\nIt's important to discuss this result with your clinician, who can consider all your health information and provide personalized guidance.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
